[Applause]  [Applause]  hey guys it's me Peter Brown reading  Justin Clark's review of Mario Tennis  aces when the credits rolled on Mario  Tennis aces adventure mode I vowed to  never laugh at a tennis player having an  ugly male down on the court I had felt  the volcanic surge of adrenaline that  comes when a rally has gone too long  I knew the sense of high alert while  trying to suss out which corner of the  court an opponent is going to attack  next  I have spliced and invented new curse  words to mutter when a ball goes out of  bounds  offbeat stages and creative use of  characters from the Mario vs. ensure  that you'll never lose sight of simply  having fun but don't let that adorable  exterior trick you  Asus takes it's unorthodox tennis very  seriously  [Applause]  Mario Tennis has renewed vigor is driven  by a suite of new mechanics that force  you to make pivotal risk/reward  decisions special shots are now tied to  a meter that fills a little with every  shot fired back of your opponent more so  if you're able to charge your swing  ahead of time once the energy meter is  at least 1/3 full a bar landing on your  side of the court will be forecast by a  glowing star initiating a special swing  while on a star activates a first-person  view that lets you aim a powerful zone  shop and when the meter is completely  full you can unleash your character's  special shot and while specials don't  unleash the kalakeya of effects they  didn't we use Mario Tennis ultra smash  they do fire a lightning fastball that  requires exacting maneuvers to return  without incurring any harm to your  racket destroy your collection of  rackets during a match and you lose  holding the r button slows down time at  the cost of meter allowing you to stroll  over and hit hard-to-reach shots or gain  a slight advantage when returning racket  breaking balls alternatively a new dodge  mechanic called trickshot allows you to  tilt the right stick in the direction of  an incoming shot and if timed right leap  across the court and smack it away at  high speed you can get away with basic  shots during simple matches but in  advanced matches the exchange of powered  up strikes feels like a breathless  symphony that requires you to be at the  top of your game and on top of your  options  even veterans of the series have a  little bit of a learning curve to  overcome but aces Adventure mode does a  good job of both entertaining you and  teaching you how and when to use your  new tools the story itself is ridiculous  but ridiculous in that very specific  quirky way intend Oh has been getting  away with for decades during the  mushroom kingdoms annual tennis  tournament and evil tennis racket yes  really named Lucien takes possession of  Luigi and flies off to find five power  stones that will help him take over the  world instead of settling for a  revolving door of opponents along the  way you're challenged to utilize aces  new mechanics in a range of unusual  scenarios an average stage might simply  challenge you to keep a rally going for  a certain length of time but bosses and  puzzle stages require a greater level of  ingenuity you have to figure out how to  disable protective barriers earning  enough energy to perform a zone shot an  aim at the right part of the court to  inflict damage bosses also initiate  hurdling challenges mid-match that  reward precise use of you're leaping  trickshot a venture mode mixes up your  objectives from one stage to the next to  ensure you're never simply going through  the motions to progress  [Music]  asus is more difficult in devious than  you might expect especially in the  latter half of adventure mode though not  required grinding through matches can't  improve your chances on the court but  win or lose urine experience points for  every match played allowing you to  improve Mario's speed power and agility  over time but no matter how much XP you  earn the only way to make it to the end  of aces campaign is to master its unique  tennis mechanics those who persevere  will find themselves better equipped and  prepared to face anything the other  modes have to offer outside of adventure  mode you'll find a rather plain  assortment of activities a bracket based  tournament mode exhibition matches  against the computer or another friend  online modes and the ability to play  doubles matches which can turn into  downright Anarchy before you know it one  surprising this step in the package is  swing mode where players can swing joy  Kahn's like proper tennis rackets  similar to we sports tennis at first it  seems odd that this control scheme is  isolated to a specific mode but within a  minute or two it's obvious why  playing with joy Kahn's feels too  imprecise and even just executing a  simple backhand was a twitchy comedy of  errors it's too bad that the motion  controls seem to fall apart so easily  but considering that and it's probably  best that the option is siloed away odds  are most players will spend the majority  of their time the long run playing aces  online an experience that can be pretty  hit or miss depending on how you're  matched up the lack of stat tracking  means that you have no way to visually  gauge how skilled your opponent may be  prior to accepting a match and as such  lopsided matches are commonplace casual  players do have the option of limiting  matches to simple gameplay which removes  special mechanics and hazard type stages  from the mix even without the  exhilaration of special shots there's  still something relaxing about  occasionally fooling around with a  no-frills version of Mario Tennis  however even this approach winds up  feeling flawed due to the lack of  options that are freely configurable in  local multiplayer matches including the  number of racquets per player the type  of stages you play on and the duration  of a battle online free play matches are  also locked into tiebreaker rules for  points instead of full-blown tennis sets  which goes a little too far in whittling  back the game's death tournament mode is  even more limited doing away with the  option to play using simple rules there  also isn't any incentive for success no  rewards waiting at the end of a brutal  five match gauntlet and frankly people  who persevere deserves something that  may change in the coming months with  multiple character slots marked as being  reserved  for participating in tournaments every  month but as of the time of this writing  even that comes off as the smallest  token still it speaks volumes that even  these limitations don't entirely dampen  my enthusiasm for aces the tetris effect  is in full swing here days after the  credits rolled I still crave the  satisfying thwack from a Power Shot  mentally replay matches and imagine how  I might do things differently given a  bit more focus and know how Mario Tennis  Aces does what the series has done best  and for the most part improves what it's  rarely gotten right before  [Music]  [Music]

